<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manghud</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mangit tribe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manghud, or Mangit, were a Mongol tribe that became Turkic-speaking in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Under the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> around 1400 they formed the core of the Nogai Horde, and other Mangit clans joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr Khan</a>. A Mangit family later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit dynasty</a>, which ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> until 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manghud.jpg" alt="Manghud" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Prokudin-Gorsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prokudin-Gorskii-19-v2_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Manghud belonged to the Urud-Manghud federation of the Mongolian plateau, and ancient sources derive them from the Kiyat Mongols. The Persian historian Rashid-al-Din Hamadani, who chronicled the Mongols, claimed that many old Mongol clans, the Manghud among them, were founded by members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a>.</p><p>Some Manghud warriors supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, while a body of them resisted his rise to power. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> expanded, the Manghud were carried westward with many other Mongol tribes. The clan name was also used for Mongol vanguard troops, though sources differ widely on the military units called mangudai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> the Manghud supported Nogai, who died in 1299, and formed a horde of their own, semi-independent of the khans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>. After Nogai's death most of their warriors entered the service of Tokhta Khan. Edigu, who lived from 1352 to 1419 and was the son of the noble Kutlukiya, led the eastern begs and by the end of the 14th century dominated the Golden Horde.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> guarded the northern borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> and Crimean khanates and by organised raids into the northern steppes prevented Russian and Lithuanian settlement. Many entered the service of the Crimean khan and contributed to the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>. About 1630 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, migrating from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, drove the Nogais out; they fled to the plains of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and a small part joined the Kazakh Khanate.</p><p>Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghits</a> had been settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a>, which remained their base under the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Their power there grew in the early 18th century through the office of ataliq to the khan. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747 the ataliq Muhammad Rahim Bi had the ruling Abu al-Fayz Khan and his young son killed. The family ruled behind the scenes until the 1780s, when the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad</a> declared himself ruler openly. Not being descendants of Genghis Khan, the Manghits took the Islamic title of emir, not khan, and based their legitimacy on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The last emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-alim-khan">Mohammed Alim Khan</a>, was ousted by the Soviet Red Army in September 1920 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Whether his dynasty was of Mongol or of plain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> origin is disputed. The Nogai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpak peoples</a> are counted among the descendants of the Manghud, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghits</a> are found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>, as well as among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manghud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nogai Horde grew from the <b>Mangit tribe</b> led by Edigu, the emir who dominated the Golden Horde around 1400.</blockquote><a href="/state/nogai-horde">Nogai Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu'l-Khayr ruled a confederation of Turkic tribes, including the Qungrat, <b>Mangit</b> and Kiyat, known collectively as Uzbeks.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
